concept qualified_kinds {
  name     = "Qualified Block Kinds"
  summary  = "Block and table kinds are namespace-scoped; a ::-qualified kind picks a schema from a specific namespace."
  related  = [namespaces, use_declarations, imports, schema]
  audience = :both
  body {
    h2 "Qualified block kinds"
    p "Block (and table) kinds are namespace-scoped too. A `::`-qualified kind at the instance site selects the `@block` declaration from that namespace, even when a local declaration shadows the bare kind."
    code "wcl" { source = <<'SRC'
import <wdoc.wcl>

// A local @block("process") shadows the bare kind...
@block("process") type MyProcess { @inline(0) text: utf8  cost: i64 }

process "mine" { cost = 3 }          // -> MyProcess (local wins)
wdoc::process "theirs" { }           // -> wdoc's Process, explicitly
SRC
    }
    h2 "How bare names resolve"
    p "A bare kind prefers a declaration in the referencing file's own namespace; otherwise it falls back to an imported one. So a user `@block(\"process\")` deterministically shadows a library's. Two same-kind declarations in the **same** namespace are an error; the same kind across different namespaces is fine — disambiguate at the instance with `::`."
    callout "import vs namespace vs use" { class = ["info"]  body = "An import decides which files participate. The imported file's namespace decides what its declarations are called, and your use declarations (or :: qualifiers) decide how you refer to them." }
  }
}
